00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:04.550 align:start position:0%
 
hey everyone i'm john coogan and welcome  to the third installment of my guide to  startup fundraising  last week i talked about the different  types of investors and how to decide  which ones might be ideal for you  today i'm going to talk about how to  manage a fundraising process and  actually meet prospective investors  as always the best way to raise money is  to build a great business  and if your company is making its  customers happy growing the customer  base significantly every month  and has a giant pool of potential  customers it'll be easy to meet  investors and they will be fighting to  get time on your calendar  let's start with the founding team's  role during fundraising since you're  optimizing for growth  not strictly fundraising it's important  to avoid letting your whole team get  sucked into the fundraising process  designate one founder to lead the  process and let the other founders stay  focused on growth so the company doesn't  stagnate while you're raising money  the quickest way to close an investor is  to show big growth in between two  meetings  the ceo should always lead the  fundraising process even if  all founders are equal shareholders this  is because investors expect the ceo to  set the vision for the company and they  want to hear about that vision directly  but that doesn't mean that the other  founders can't help out everyone should  introduce any potential investors that  they know to the ceo  and be available for in-person meetings  with investors who plan on writing large  checks and want to meet the whole team  even though it might feel like you can  make fundraising go faster by having  every founder work on it full time  the cost of slower growth and the  inevitable confusion around what  allocations have been promised to what  investors make the ceo-led approach  vastly preferable  now that you have the ceo clearly  leading fundraising you need to focus on  going as broad as possible with your  outreach  instead of finding one investor having  an introductory meeting then a follow-up  meeting then getting a decision  and then finding the next investor to  talk to aim to talk to all of your  investor connections  simultaneously inevitably you will need  to do some prioritization so when it  comes to prioritizing meetings during  fundraising you should think about the  expected value associated with each  investor  an angel investor who's very likely to  move quickly but only writes small  checks  might have the same expected value as a  vc who could lead the round  but is less likely to do so ideally you  should spend your time with investors  who move quickly and lead rounds  and always try and avoid small angels  who require lots of meetings to get to a  decision  that's a total red flag if you're having  trouble assessing investors before you  reach out to them  be sure to check references with other  entrepreneurs that they've worked with  in the past  and get a feel for who would have the  highest expected value for your round  i find that setting up a spreadsheet to  track outreach is really helpful at this  stage  you don't need anything fancy like a  full crm just a list of everyone who  you're in contact with and where they  are in the pipeline  here's a template that i like to use  while fundraising start by listing every  investor you know along with anyone who  works with investors regularly  as you take meetings with investors  update the sheet to reflect whether or  not they have committed  and how much you expect to receive from  them be sure to add notes and to track  when you ask for additional intros  introductions are the best way to meet  investors and there are a few different  varieties of intros  the best introduction is always one  between a well-known investor  who is committed to your round and then  a new potential investor  after that getting an intro to an  investor through a founder who has  raised money from that investor before  is great the startup world also has a  few different types of super connectors  like lawyers and reporters  and they can connect you to investors  too although those referrals usually  hold a little less weight  angel list and other fundraising sites  can help you connect with a broader set  of investors  but it can be hard to get initial  commitments there and you'll have a much  easier time on those platforms if you  already have a big name  investor attached to your round although  it can be tempting to take every intro  that's offered to you  you should avoid taking intros from  investors who have passed on your round  since that's a negative signal  if an investor passes and offers to make  an introduction to someone  make a note of that person's name and  then make a connection through a  different channel  if you can't get an introduction or  don't know any investors cold outreach  can work surprisingly well when done  carefully  you never want to spam investors and  send generic emails  instead focus on finding investors who  are specifically interested in your  industry  and send them a thoughtful email tell  them where your company stands today  and why it's a fit with their most  recent investment thesis  most investors have blogs or twitter  accounts where they talk about what  they're interested in  and this is a great way to make sure  that they can be valuable to your  company and that they're worth your time  to try and pitch  investors are hoping to find amazing  companies that no one knows about  so don't be intimidated by cold emailing  investors who you know would love what  you're working on  another way to increase the  effectiveness of your cold outreach is  to scale back what you're asking for  your goal is to get a meeting scheduled  where you can give a full pitch  but offering to start with a 15 minute  phone call or even just a short back and  forth over email  is much easier for an investor to say  yes to investors hear pitches all day  and don't want to waste their time  so allowing them to start with a short  call or exchange of questions  reassures them that you can concisely  describe your business  it can be super helpful to start  developing relationships well before you  plan on fundraising  one of the best ways to do this is to  engage with investors and other experts  in your field on social media  sites like twitter medium substack and  quora are all common publishing outlets  for investors  well you shouldn't waste too much time  browsing it's great to stay on top of  what the vc community is thinking about  your industry  since you're working on your startup  every day you'll often have additional  context and be able to add to the  conversation  establishing yourself as an industry  insider can help you connect with  investors organically  it's a delicate balance though you don't  want to pick fights or come off as a  cocky know-it-all  so stay positive and informative in some  cases if no one is talking about your  industry at all  it might make sense for you to start the  conversation yourself that's exactly  what we did at soylent  in 2013 silicon valley wasn't talking  about food nutrition or even consumer  package goods very much  so our ceo rob reinhart started writing  about our product on his personal blog  his writing did well on hacker news and  he was able to gain a following  this made conversations with investors  much easier because there was already a  discussion about the industry going on  and we were at the center of it once  you've figured out how to connect to an  investor  either through an introduction or cold  outreach you need to pitch your business  in the most succinct way possible  this is typically called an elevator  pitch and should cover the key points  you want an investor to remember  you need to highlight what you do how  fast you're growing what makes your  company different from competitors  and how large the market is cold emails  and warm intros should include  at most a few sentences about your  company so you need to make them count  avoid jargon heavy lingo and focus on  describing your business in plain  english  there are a few good ways to summarize  what you do and ultimately this will  depend on your specific business  but here are a few templates that can be  helpful you can use an analogy to  compare yourself to a popular business  that everyone understands  this usually takes the form of x for y  and there are a bunch of popular  examples of this  youtube was originally flickr for video  airbnb  was ebay for physical space and baidu  was google for china  just make sure that investors actually  know the companies that you are drawing  comparisons with  if you use some obscure company that no  one knows about you'll just confuse  everyone  another common format is focusing on the  problem and the solution  so the stripe founders might say we  allow developers to integrate payments  without headaches  before stripe it was common knowledge  that payment integrations were  notoriously difficult  so clearly describing the value  headache-free payments  in relation to the users developers  makes it easy to understand what the  company does  in addition to your elevator pitch  you'll also want to include a link to  your product or landing page  but you don't need to send a deck  immediately if an investor asks to see a  deck after scheduling a meeting  it's fine to send one in advance so they  can ask better questions  but avoid sending the deck before  scheduling the meeting since you don't  want them making the decision  on whether or not to meet you based on  your deck alone  once you've got a meeting on the books  you'll need to be ready to share your  vision for the company through a deck  having a clear slide deck will make sure  you hit all the key questions and have  some extra data or examples that can be  referenced by the investor after the  meeting  in my next video i'll go through how to  build a great fundraising deck  so be sure to hit the subscribe button  investor outreach is just the first step  in pitching your company  so cast a wide net and network as  broadly as possible  preparing a long list of potential  investors will make the rest of the  process  much easier this allows you to run an  efficient and competitive round  so really spend some time generating  good leads  if you think i missed anything please  leave me a comment below i try and  respond to every question i get  and if you enjoyed this video a like  rating is always appreciated  thanks for watching

